FINAL ANSWER:
**The bus factor of `lib/` is 1.** One person, Troy Goode, holds more than half of the surviving lines, so losing that one person would leave most of the code with nobody who wrote it.

`lib/` is a single file, `lib/index.js`: 214 non-blank lines, 238 counting blank lines. All figures below come from surviving-lines (git blame with `-w -M -C`).

**Who holds the lines.** Troy Goode appears under two addresses, which look like the same person. Counting them as one is a guess from the name and addresses; nothing has been written to `.mailmap`.

| Author | Non-blank lines | Counting blank lines | Last commit |
|---|---|---|---|
| Troy Goode (both addresses) | 144 (67.3%) | 159 (66.8%) | 2026-04-10 |
| ↳ <email redacted> | 132 (61.7%) | 147 (61.8%) | 2019-05-27 |
| ↳ troy@laptop.example | 12 (5.6%) | 12 (5.0%) | 2026-04-10 |
| Douglas Christopher Wilson | 20 (9.3%) | 22 (9.2%) | 2021-09-07 |
| Alejandro Caravaca Puchades | 14 (6.5%) | 16 (6.7%) | 2015-05-28 |
| LuisEGR / Raymond Feng | 6 each (2.8%) | 6 (2.5%) / 7 (2.9%) | 2021 / 2015 |
| 18 more authors | rest | | |

**Bus factor figures.** The bus factor here is the smallest number of authors whose surviving lines add up to 50% of the lines.
- **50% threshold:** 1 (Troy Goode) on both line bases. It is still 1 without the merge: the gmail address alone holds 61.7% of non-blank lines and 61.8% counting blank lines.
- **80% threshold:** 3 authors with the merge, 4 without.
- **Active people only:** 1. This is counted only on the 144 lines held by people who committed in the 365 days before 2026-04-12. Troy Goode is the only one, and the other 17 authors (32.7% of the lines) have not committed since.

**If Troy Goode leaves** (`--without "Troy Goode"`):
- 144 of 214 lines (67.3%) lose their author.
- `lib/index.js` gets flagged as a file nobody remaining would know. The best remaining author is Douglas Christopher Wilson with 9.3%, and he hasn't committed since 2021.
- The bus factor of what's left is 3. That figure is counted only on the remaining lines.
- The people who would inherit the most are Douglas Christopher Wilson (20 lines), Alejandro Caravaca Puchades (14) and LuisEGR (6). None of them has committed in the last year.

So to your question: **one departure is enough.** Troy Goode is both the main source of knowledge and the only active contact. That makes `lib/` a single point of failure, but the tool doesn't call it orphaned yet, because its top owner is still active.

**Adjustments in the counts:**
- **Reformat commit left out:** commit `62628c0` ("style: reformat js…") rewrote 1,016 lines in 4 files, so its lines go back to their earlier authors. Counting the reformat anyway, Troy Goode would hold 123 lines (57.5%) and the bus factor would still be 1.
- **Possible mechanical commit kept:** `fcd2b56` (the ESLint switch) also looks mechanical but was kept in the counts. It holds only 5 of Troy's lines, so it can't change the bus factor.
- **Lines credited to the author, not the committer:** 11 lines committed by Troy Goode are credited to Alejandro Caravaca Puchades, and 6 committed by Wilson are credited to LuisEGR.
- **Same address, two names:** Alejandro Caravaca Puchades also appears as "alex94puchades" under the same address, and is counted once.

Two limits on these numbers. They measure whose code is still there, not who actually understands it: reviewers and past maintainers may know `lib/index.js` without holding any lines in it. And the history starts with an import in 2013-01-30, so any code older than that is credited to whoever imported it.
